Drop the Secure Engine's HAL_GetTick and HAL_Delay, and keep the rest of
se_utils.c.

ST's own comment on HAL_GetTick says what it is for: "This function
overwrites the one declared as __weak in HAL ... time is counted without
using SysTick timer interrupts."  Behind the firewall the Secure Engine
cannot reach the application's tick, so it derives one from SysTick->VAL by
hand.  This rig links one ELF with the firewall off
(B_L475E_IOT01A/2_Images_SECoreBin/Inc/se_low_level.h), so ST's HAL supplies
both symbols and keeping se_utils' pair is a duplicate definition.

WHY NOT SIMPLY LEAVE THE FILE OUT, which is what this rig did at first: it
also defines SE_SetSystemCoreClock, which se_callgate.c:410 calls and
nothing else provides.  Excluding the file links everything else and fails
on that one symbol.

The behaviour the SE gets instead is the HAL's: a tick incremented by the
SysTick interrupt rather than one computed from the counter's value.  For
this rig that is the better of the two -- it is the same tick the rest of
the bootloader reads -- and neither is in the timed path, which is counted
in DWT cycles and not in milliseconds.

Applied by the Makefile's SE_STAGE_RULE with -F0.
--- se_utils.c
+++ se_utils.c
@@ -58,6 +58,7 @@
   m_uSE_SystemCoreClock = uSystemCoreClock;
 }
 
+#if !defined(PORT_SINGLE_IMAGE)
 /**
   * @brief Provide a tick value in millisecond.
   * @note This function overwrites the one declared as __weak in HAL.
@@ -112,6 +113,7 @@
   {
   }
 }
+#endif /* !PORT_SINGLE_IMAGE */
 
 /**
   * @}
